    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d645">

                <pb facs="0632" n="534" xml:id="pg534"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Choate.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-12-21"><hi rendition="#italic">December 21,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 262.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose for your information copy of
                        correspondence,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d645-f1"/> as indicated below, in
                    regard to the reported seizure by Her Majesty’s cruiser <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Partridge</hi> of the British steamers <hi rendition="#italic">Mashona</hi>
                    and <hi rendition="#italic">Maria</hi> off the east coast of Africa, portions of
                    the cargoes of which, consisting of general merchandise, are the property of
                    Messrs. Flint, Eddy &amp; Co.</p>
                <p>You are instructed to bring the matter to the attention of the British
                    Government, and to inquire as to the circumstances and legality of the seizure.
                    If it was illegal, you will request prompt action and restitution in the
                    case.</p>
                <p>The United States consul at Lourenço Marquez will be instructed to use all proper
                    efforts to protect the rights of Messrs. Flint, Eddy &amp; Co.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d645-f1" n="1" xml:id="d645-f1-content">
                <p>(Inclosures:) Messrs. Hopkins &amp; Hopkins to Mr. Hay, December 12, 1899, and
